1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,992
Before: Look at this java for a moment.
After: Look at this java for a moment. The
freight-rate service had fourteen @Value
injections across three classes, three of
them misspelled in YAML with silent
defaults.

2
00:00:14,112 --> 00:00:23,544
Typed FreightRatesProperties binding turns
a prefix into one object, validates it,
and fails startup when required fields are
missing — if you enable that validation.

3
00:00:23,664 --> 00:00:34,656
Scattered @Value strings drift; a single
properties type is a contract with ops.
@ConfigurationProperties binds Environment
properties to a structured Java type.

4
00:00:34,776 --> 00:00:47,088
Enable with @EnableConfigurationProperties
(FreightRatesProperties.class) or
@ConfigurationPropertiesScan, and register
the type as a bean (@Component or via
@EnableConfigurationProperties).

5
00:00:47,207 --> 00:00:58,775
Boot's relaxed binding maps
freight.rates.base-per-kg,
FREIGHT_RATES_BASE_PER_KG, and
freight.rates.basePerKg to the same record
component.

6
00:00:58,896 --> 00:01:13,680
That relaxation is why env vars in
Kubernetes and keys in YAML can disagree
in punctuation yet agree in meaning. Look
at this example. freight: Look at this
java for a moment. Walk the after side.

7
00:01:13,799 --> 00:01:26,015
prefix = "freight.rates" scopes binding to
that subtree — unrelated freight.routing.*
keys stay out. Record components declare
the shape; Boot binds by constructor for
records.

8
00:01:26,135 --> 00:01:31,655
RateCardService depends on one
collaborator instead of four @Value
parameters.

9
00:01:31,775 --> 00:01:43,343
quote reads basePerKg(), applies fuel,
enforces minCharge — business math stays
readable because configuration access is
not interleaved with @Value noise. Runtime
during refresh.

10
00:01:43,463 --> 00:01:55,679
A binder / ConfigurationPropertiesBindingP
ostProcessor path creates or populates the
properties bean from the Environment.
Conversion services turn YAML strings into
BigDecimal and double.

11
00:01:55,799 --> 00:02:01,655
If you annotate the type with @Validated
and place @NotNull / @Positive on
components,

12
00:02:01,775 --> 00:02:11,543
a BindException / constraint violation
fails the context before traffic —
misspelled base-per-kilo leaves basePerKg
null and startup dies instead of quoting
with zero.

13
00:02:11,663 --> 00:02:19,175
Without validation, a missing key may
leave null and fail later inside quote
with a NullPointerException on the first
request — worse.

14
00:02:19,295 --> 00:02:30,623
@Value still works for true one-offs;
properties classes win for groups that
evolve together and for IDE metadata via
spring-boot-configuration-processor
(optional dependency that generates
hints).

15
00:02:30,743 --> 00:02:42,455
Failure mode from the fourteen-@Value era:
YAML key freight.rates.fuel-percent while
code asks fuel-pct. @Value with a default
silently uses the default; quotes
undercharge fuel for weeks.

16
00:02:42,575 --> 00:02:49,463
Symptom: finance reconciles against a
spreadsheet and finds a constant
undercharge equal to the missing
surcharge.

17
00:02:49,584 --> 00:02:58,391
With @ConfigurationProperties + validation
on fuelPct, the bad key leaves the field
unset/invalid and refresh fails in CI.

18
00:02:58,512 --> 00:03:08,111
Another failure: forgetting to register
the properties type as a bean —
RateCardService cannot autowire
FreightRatesProperties, startup
NoSuchBeanDefinitionException.

19
00:03:08,231 --> 00:03:22,199
Fix: @EnableConfigurationProperties or
component-scan the type. Trade-offs.
Properties classes add a type and a
registration line; they remove stringly-
typed sprawl and enable validation and
documentation.

20
00:03:22,319 --> 00:03:33,503
Mutable JavaBean-style properties with
setters are easy for binding and awkward
for immutability;
records/@ConstructorBinding favor
immutability and slightly stricter Boot
version awareness.

21
00:03:33,623 --> 00:03:48,048
Nested prefixes
(freight.rates.lanes[0].from) model lists
and maps well — overusing deep nests makes
YAML harder for humans than flat keys with
clear names. Relaxed binding deserves one
concrete freight walkthrough.

22
00:03:48,168 --> 00:04:06,480
Environment may expose
FREIGHT_RATES_BASE_PER_KG=1.40 from a
Kubernetes Secret while application.yml
still says base-per-kg: 1.25. The binder
normalizes both to the basePerKg
component; the env var wins by precedence.

23
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:14,088
@Value("${freight.rates.base-per-kg}")
also benefits from relaxed binding for env
vars,

24
00:04:14,208 --> 00:04:22,487
but fourteen separate @Value sites still
cannot validate the group as one object or
generate a single metadata file for IDE
completion.

25
00:04:22,608 --> 00:04:29,903
That is why the freight team moved: not
because @Value is illegal, but because the
*set* of rates is one configuration
surface.

26
00:04:30,024 --> 00:04:43,271
Misconception unique to configuration
properties: "Immutable
@ConfigurationProperties records cannot
work because Boot needs setters." Boot
binds constructor parameters of records
and constructor-binding types.

27
00:04:43,391 --> 00:04:50,112
Setters are one style, not the only style.
Rates are typed and validated in one
place.

28
00:04:50,231 --> 00:05:01,319
Ops still needs to change those values in
Kubernetes without rebuilding the JAR —
ConfigMaps, Secrets, and override order —
the external configuration story that sits
on top of the same binder.
